📁 test/ — Subsystem and Integration Tests
This folder contains individual test programs to validate functionality of hardware interfaces and subsystems within the ROBOT_COOK autonomous robotics project. Each .cpp file is written as a standalone PlatformIO test sketch targeting the ESP32 microcontroller.

🧪 Test File Summary
File	Description
drivetest.cpp	Tests motor driving in all directions and validates rotation logic using drive.h.
reflectancedrivetest.cpp	Integrates sensor reading with line-following motion control to dynamically center the robot on the black tape.
reflectancetest.cpp	Reports raw reflectance sensor values and direction classifications (e.g., LEFT, RIGHT, CENTERED).
servotest.cpp	Sweeps servo angles through a range to validate positioning and tracking of servo claw movement.
microswitchtest.cpp	Verifies microswitch functionality and pin readings, printing results over serial.

🧰 How to Run
Open the test file you want to run (e.g. servotest.cpp) in VSCode.

Flash it to the ESP32 board:

pio run --target upload --environment <your_env_name>

Monitor serial output:

pio device monitor

Observe the behavior of the corresponding hardware module (motors, servos, sensors, etc.).

🧠 Why This Matters
These tests ensure that:

Hardware components are wired and functioning correctly

Pin mappings match the definitions in config.h

Logic modules (drive, sensors, servos) behave as expected in isolation

Bugs can be caught before integrating into full task routines like burger2()

📌 Notes
These are not PlatformIO unit tests (with unity.h), but rather standalone integration test sketches.

Future improvement: adopt PlatformIO Unit Testing for CI-style validation.

Test output is written to serial (115200 baud) and should be monitored live during execution.
